ce:section.ce:para:
To balance computational efficiency with accuracy, the total number of particle bins (
                              m
                           ) was set to 30. This binning choice ensures a sufficient resolution to capture particle distribution dynamics effectively. Any number above 30 will lead to significantly higher computational time. As can be seen in Fig. 9, although increasing the number of bins improves accuracy, using around 30 bins strikes a balance by being sufficiently close to larger values while significantly reducing computational time. The initial condition was constructed by assuming that all particles present, based on the specific protein concentration and reaction volume, act as primary particles in the experiment. Further details are provided in the supplementary material (Table S2). The ODE system was then solved using the solve_ivp function from Python’s SciPy library, with the LSODA method chosen due to its robust handling of stiffness, which enhances solution stability and convergence. Further details about the solver are provided in the supplementary material (section 2.2).

In this work, we have explored dynamical modeling and ML to elucidate the mechanisms of protein precipitation from process operating conditions. The PBM model allows us to create a dependence between its critical parameters to the measured PSD. However, due to the computational and experimental effort to obtain these parameters, we have presented a surrogate modeling strategy to predict the PBM parameters from the process operating conditions data. We have also shown the use of measured distributions from the FBRM probe for the amount of particles in a length scale to get regressed PBM parameters. Combining a mechanistic model for PBM given the critical process model parameters of maximum collision frequency (


                              α


                              max


                     ) and breakage coefficient (
                        E
                     ), with a surrogate model to correlate the operational data temperature, rotation speed, protein concentration, salt concentration, showcases a foundation for future work to realize a hybrid, mechanistic and data-driven model. In this study, the modeling approach has been presented to address the recovery of proteins like lysosymes. PBMs are utilized to predict PSDs, and process operating conditions are correlated with PBM parameters, in this work. The combination of PBM with a surrogate model (ANN) helps find the parameters of the mechanistic dynamical model. The surrogate model also aids in quantifying mechanistically-plausible dependencies of the process operating conditions on PBM parameters of interest .
